        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          STS-85 marked the 23rd flight of the Space Shuttle Discovery and the 86th flight of the Space Shuttle program.  The Shuttle crew included Commander Curtis L. Brown, Jr., Pilot Kent V. Rominger, Mission Specialists N. Jan Davis, Robert L. Curbeam, and Stephen K. Robinson, and Payload Specialist Bjarni Tryggvason. Ground fog at Kennedy Space Center slightly delayed launch, but burned off as the sun rose allowing the countdown to continue.  Discovery launched on August 7, 1997, at 10:41:00 a.m. EDT.<P>The primary objective of this mission was the deployment and retrieval of a satellite, called the Cryogenic Infrared Spectrometers and Telescopes for the Atmosphere-Shuttle Pallet Satellite-2 (CRISTA-SPAS-2), designed to study Earth's middle atmosphere.  This was the second flight of the CRISTA-SPAS-2 satellite on the Space Shuttle (previously flown on STS-66 in 1994) and is the fourth mission in a cooperative venture between the German Space Agency (DARA) and NASA. <P>During the flight, Discovery's robot arm was used to deploy the CRISTA-SPAS payload for nine days of free flight. CRISTA-SPAS consisted of three telescopes and four spectrometers that measured trace gases and dynamics of the Earth's middle atmosphere.  The Shuttle Pallet Satellite (SPAS) on which the scientific instruments are mounted is a self-contained space vehicle that provides power, command, control and communication with Discovery during free flight.  After nine days of flight, the robot arm was used to retrieve the CRISTA-SPAS satellite. <P>STS-85 was the tenth flight of the Biological Research in a Canister (BRIC) small payloads project.  BRIC payloads consist of passive experiments contained within a metal canister placed in a middeck locker with foam padding used to dampen vibrations.  The BRIC-10 payload was specifically designed to study the effects of microgravity on growth, development, and metabolic processes in Arabidopsis thaliana and tobacco seeds.  The investigation used the specimens to identify and clone genes whose expression was altered when grown in microgravity.  BRIC experiment specimens were exposed to the temperatures, carbon dioxide, oxygen, atmospheric pressure and humidity conditions of the middeck.<P>Additional life sciences experiments performed during the STS-85 mission were those classified as Detailed Supplementary Objectives (DSOs).  A DSO is a NASA-sponsored investigation performed by Space Shuttle crewmembers, who serve as the test subjects.  These studies are designed to require minimal crew time, power and stowage.  Biomedical DSOs focus on operational concerns, including space motion sickness, cardiovascular deconditioning, muscle loss, changes in coordination and balance strategies, radiation exposure, pharmacokinetics and changes in the body's biochemistry.  <P>Other payloads flown on STS-85 were the Manipulator Flight Demonstrator, a Japanese Space Agency experiment to prepare for the International Space Station (ISS); Technology Applications and Science payload; International Extreme Ultraviolet Hitchhiker; Microgravity Vibration Isolation Mount, designed to isolate experiments from disturbances created by thruster firings or crew activity on the ISS; Southwest Ultraviolet Imaging System, to observe comet Hale-Bopp;  Protein Crystallization Apparatus for Microgravity; and two Get Away Special experiments, Hearts in Space and Bean Seed Root Growth.<P>This mission was scheduled to end August 18, 1997, by landing at Kennedy Space Center (KSC) in Florida but the attempt was waived off due to low fog at the Shuttle Landing Facility.  Because opportunities for landing at Edward's Air Force Base in California were not considered, Discovery landed the following day at KSC.<END>
